Blackdrift: keep no Gecko crash dumps on the device.

Why: when a content process (or the app) crashes, Gecko writes a minidump
(.dmp, about 1.5 MB) and a .extra file into the app's private
files/mozilla/Crash Reports/pending/. The .extra holds the crashed page's
URL. Blackdrift never uploads them (0102), so they are leftover browsing data
that outlives the session and the "erase" button
(docs/telemetry-network-check.md, Findings 2).

Gecko keeps writing the dumps on purpose. Without a crash handler
GeckoRuntime sets MOZ_CRASHREPORTER_DISABLE=1 and no dump is written, but
GeckoView then cannot tell a crash from an out-of-memory kill: it sends
onCrash only when the dead process left a dump ID
(GeckoViewContent.sys.mjs, "ipc:content-shutdown"), otherwise onKill. A
crash would then skip the tab-crash screen and reload the tab at once, in a
loop for a page that crashes every time. The switch also lives in Gecko's
prebuilt code, which an artifact build cannot change.

So the new CrashDumpCleaner deletes the files (not the directories) in
Crash Reports/pending, Crash Reports/events and files/minidumps:

- on every app start, in the main process (covers a crash of the app
  itself);
- after every tab crash (CrashAction), so the tab-crash screen still shows
  and its "Close tab" still recovers;
- whenever the browser store is left with no tab: the erase button, the
  notification's erase action, the erase shortcuts, removing Blackdrift from
  recents, and closing the last tab.

The deletion runs on Dispatchers.IO in the application scope. New unit test
CrashDumpCleanerTest.

Cut against: mozilla-firefox/firefox@6181e6b5f28a8fbae1d5c3ef6f6626b5a2180213 (Firefox 159.0.1).

